# config/versions.env - Versions of the shared local toolchain
#
# What is NOT here: anything a network is made of. Node binaries, runtimes, the
# service binaries and the bite tool are declared per network — with their release
# and version — in networks/<name>.json, because two networks legitimately run
# different builds of the same component. See networks/README.md.
#
# What is here: tools that are the same whichever network is running.

# =============================================================================
# The engine's own release channels
# =============================================================================
# The engine builds no binaries or runtimes — every one is pinned below and fetched from
# the release of the repo that owns it. Two channels are its own:
#
#   PPN_TAG        stable semver releases: the deployable dist tarball, its changelog and
#                  the Docker image. These own GitHub `latest`.
#   PPN_BITE_TAG   one rolling pre-release carrying the fork bundles (one per pre-bitten
#                  network), refreshed by the nightly bite. Pre-releases never become
#                  `latest`, so bundles can churn nightly without moving what deploys resolve.
#
# Bundles come only from PPN_BITE_TAG: a stable release carries none, and there is nothing
# to walk back through — the rolling tag is either current or the nightly failed, in which
# case FRESH_BITE=1 bites the source directly.
PPN_REPO="${PPN_REPO:-paritytech/previewnet-engine}"
PPN_TAG="${PPN_TAG:-latest}"
PPN_BITE_TAG="${PPN_BITE_TAG:-bites}"

# =============================================================================
# Node binaries, runtimes, service binaries → networks/<name>.json
# =============================================================================
# Each chain declares the binary it runs and the release it comes from; genesis
# networks also declare each chain's runtime WASM. `make fetch` downloads exactly
# that set, into bin/ (previewnet) or bin/<network>/.
#
# Only genesis (previewnet) fetches runtimes at all. Every source they come from is public
# now — previewnet's Asset Hub and People runtimes moved to paritytech/individuality-community
# — so the only thing a fetch still needs a token for is PPN's *own* release assets (the DotNS
# genesis artifacts), which live in this private repo.

# =============================================================================
# Zombienet CLI
# =============================================================================
ZOMBIENET_REPO="paritytech/zombienet-sdk"
ZOMBIENET_VERSION="v0.4.15"

# =============================================================================
# Doppelganger — only needed to CREATE a fork bundle → networks/<name>.json
# =============================================================================
# Pinned per network as bite.doppelganger, since the build has to be able to
# execute the runtimes of the network being bitten. Restoring a published bundle
# (`make start FORK=1`) needs none of it: the forked network runs on the regular
# node binaries.

# =============================================================================
# Kubo IPFS
# =============================================================================
KUBO_REPO="ipfs/kubo"
KUBO_VERSION="v0.39.0"

# =============================================================================
# Identity Backend (see docs/DEVICE-UNIQUENESS-BACKEND.md)
# =============================================================================
# PPN used to compile this itself, because the repo published container images only. As of
# v0.2.0 it publishes per-target binary tarballs — one binary per triple, with every service a
# `--role` of it — so `ppn fetch` takes the binary from upstream and `build-identity-backend`
# is gone from release.yml. With it went the failure mode where a broken backend build
# published a PPN release whose backend services were simply absent.
#
# Public, as of the -community repo below, so no token is needed to read it — which is why
# the deploy's GitHub App no longer lists it and PPN_GITHUB_TOKEN no longer has to see it.
# The API reference `/docs` serves comes from the same tag, out of the tree
# (docs/api-reference/) — the release carries the tarballs and SHA256SUMS, and nothing else.
#
# Moving this pin is not only a version change. v0.3.0 renamed the binary `ibv2` -> `dub` and
# its assets with it, renamed the identity service (and its roles and config keys) to
# device-attestation, and deleted the dim-tickets service — a role a deployment still lists is
# a boot failure, not a warning. `ppn fetch` and packages/network-config/src/dub.ts read this
# tag but not its release notes, so a bump needs both changed together.
#
# v0.4.0 is that repo's first public release, and its version does not continue the private
# 0.1-0.3 series: upstream skipped to 0.4.0 so these binaries cannot be confused with the
# public `docker.io/paritytech/device-uniqueness-backend` images tagged 0.1-0.3, which were
# built from older source. It kept the asset naming (dub-<version>-<triple>.tar.gz), the roles
# PPN spawns (all-in-one plus the three single-instance workers) and every config key in
# dub.ts — checked by booting each role against this env, not read off the release notes.
# `INVITE_INVITER_*` in upstream's .env.example is compose's host-side naming; the key the
# binary reads is still INVITER_ADDRESS.
DUB_REPO="paritytech/device-uniqueness-backend-community"
DUB_TAG="${DUB_TAG:-v0.4.0}"

# Portable PostgreSQL for the backend services. Fetched straight from upstream
# (public repo, no auth) because it is not a PPN build product. This is the
# distribution the postgresql_embedded crate uses; 16.x matches the Postgres
# version device-uniqueness-backend's own compose file pins.
POSTGRES_REPO="theseus-rs/postgresql-binaries"
POSTGRES_VERSION="16.14.0"

# =============================================================================
# DotNS - Naming Service Genesis Contracts (deployed on Asset Hub from genesis)
# =============================================================================
# Since dotns v0.5.6-rc1 the release itself carries the pallet-revive genesis
# (dotns-genesis-<tld>.json), built and address-parity-checked in dotns CI, so PPN
# fetches it instead of cloning dotns and running the deploy pipeline locally.
# Pinned to the first release that has it; move to "latest" once a full (non-rc)
# release carries the asset.
DOTNS_REPO="paritytech/dotns"
DOTNS_TAG="v0.5.7-rc1"
# Which TLD's genesis to fetch is NOT pinned here: the network descriptor's
# genesisConfig.networkSuffix is the namespace, and the DotNS TLD is the same value —
# one knob, so the two cannot drift. The artifact stamps its own TLD and injection
# asserts it against the descriptor.

# =============================================================================
# Proof of Ink Design Families (tattoo data for IPFS pinning)
# =============================================================================
# Fetched from the upstream repo, which is public now. Its v0.1.0 zip is
# byte-identical to the copy PPN used to re-publish, so the pinned CIDs hold.
DESIGN_FAMILIES_REPO="${DESIGN_FAMILIES_REPO:-paritytech/proof-of-ink-design-families}"
DESIGN_FAMILIES_VERSION="${DESIGN_FAMILIES_VERSION:-v0.1.0}"
